[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-10404"] :question/problem "What is the domain of the function  $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is an integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-10406/selection/8b56c69dec276c5e7504b921867b0a5b674416a7eebddf6b8f41f5b25ded175c" :answer/id #uuid "cfc93677-52b5-53aa-9bcc-e50761fa688d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-10406/selection/54584af94865145a8922a52a37b6cc169d94317e2fcc92ca1dd002fcabf83fdb" :answer/id #uuid "a0013cf5-eb51-5b6a-aae9-684a44a9457e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+4n\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-10406/selection/2a344dfad6d5762af74df5fa6e6bea67a0fef1e4e0d6957fdc80b403353fc08b" :answer/id #uuid "0336b4b0-cb3a-5321-8eaa-61b75d000693" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-10406/selection/0425f177a98d5043057f8dd04d35c06fc7fd1b2f3e71cb235a6c95c33dbacfa2" :answer/id #uuid "0fcac61d-695e-5554-9f4d-7de5fe867dfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-10406/selection/0511dafa692ce67a7a5a92bf2d05c9644ef78c64dd0594ba037d5204b1273be3" :answer/id #uuid "40e93def-d1d5-5414-a55a-1ddb48e21501" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-10406/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "34b2fdc7-ca35-54c9-b580-3331b57d0057" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-10406/selection/0425f177a98d5043057f8dd04d35c06fc7fd1b2f3e71cb235a6c95c33dbacfa2" :answer-field/choices ["q-10406/selection/8b56c69dec276c5e7504b921867b0a5b674416a7eebddf6b8f41f5b25ded175c" "q-10406/selection/54584af94865145a8922a52a37b6cc169d94317e2fcc92ca1dd002fcabf83fdb" "q-10406/selection/2a344dfad6d5762af74df5fa6e6bea67a0fef1e4e0d6957fdc80b403353fc08b" "q-10406/selection/0425f177a98d5043057f8dd04d35c06fc7fd1b2f3e71cb235a6c95c33dbacfa2" "q-10406/selection/0511dafa692ce67a7a5a92bf2d05c9644ef78c64dd0594ba037d5204b1273be3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-10406" :question/id #uuid "ff49aec1-dbe8-567d-97c7-2ab5d0eb085f" :question/math-academy-id "q-10406" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-10406/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd6703d2-36f6-5665-90e3-f75aea5f663b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-10406"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-29352"] :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)+2?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)+2,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is an integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-81542/selection/852693ccaf5b2b35112bc3e8ab5e6b70a5d85ba9e97e4fdfee920c6012235262" :answer/id #uuid "52b6b85b-edd5-5a10-ae7b-35a3e9f6b321" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81542/selection/11c48a094a0e45a0948d6c1f2f3840c28caaf3e5c2bddd964aaec8d7affc95d5" :answer/id #uuid "c8dc52fc-98f0-5bc9-8fe3-92389c14490e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81542/selection/6cb360ed98dfdab94d7c0cf3109825bc7ba5212d4d66fe33ded6ee01462d6eb5" :answer/id #uuid "ead94950-c0e2-5cb3-bd4e-fc64a0c04635" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81542/selection/4a64f23c9e27ea450af0c95c540553bc9512670ec9468e8d9d67f33d65d4bb2d" :answer/id #uuid "4e3bb7ed-b663-5998-9706-e51c7a8fa092" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81542/selection/12d00d8cbbf4a04c75ab1402693219a77b5a75fe9d62eb748da8bc882b535ddd" :answer/id #uuid "5fe5fc7f-e618-516e-864d-3d6f7548060b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81542/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce6064ee-6d16-5ec2-ab67-38aae0c43702" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81542/selection/12d00d8cbbf4a04c75ab1402693219a77b5a75fe9d62eb748da8bc882b535ddd" :answer-field/choices ["q-81542/selection/852693ccaf5b2b35112bc3e8ab5e6b70a5d85ba9e97e4fdfee920c6012235262" "q-81542/selection/11c48a094a0e45a0948d6c1f2f3840c28caaf3e5c2bddd964aaec8d7affc95d5" "q-81542/selection/6cb360ed98dfdab94d7c0cf3109825bc7ba5212d4d66fe33ded6ee01462d6eb5" "q-81542/selection/4a64f23c9e27ea450af0c95c540553bc9512670ec9468e8d9d67f33d65d4bb2d" "q-81542/selection/12d00d8cbbf4a04c75ab1402693219a77b5a75fe9d62eb748da8bc882b535ddd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81542" :question/id #uuid "db3f3d5c-5651-5847-bdb7-4ee59fd9d9fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-81542" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-81542/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fabd8865-cc14-508a-aa81-4ba147f7f8d4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81542"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81557/selection/513f9fe6044a27d7e7c75e28e0903cb1a8f7514cae2f17d52773451e2a33062d" :answer/id #uuid "77596efb-c13d-58ef-bf89-056eaf7245a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81557/selection/2c0500ace50320340c88d141436938707d74974b748a1ba1daa9ad6ee96b8250" :answer/id #uuid "ad233bc5-bfa0-5d47-9ba5-258600819bb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2+n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81557/selection/942a7319a5840e1944f3ffd631c86e2072937ac60deae686670df1b3fa178dfb" :answer/id #uuid "52df50b3-e220-546d-994c-1fbe30c3784b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=1+2n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81557/selection/90c96fbe037c4fb273d63857e8c5e539fea154d4e96d956c702836bbdb448824" :answer/id #uuid "b0f2dca3-3ffc-5904-b7ad-84e2397e9e07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81557/selection/b8d4d103913bf9d4df9ce0b9cd9b27cd4be59baeb236890bf50b08497b3c115e" :answer/id #uuid "c770b61f-0034-5113-b019-c22675fa17fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pi +2n\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81557/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d25ae64-a58a-5566-9862-d970b871c7d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81557/selection/942a7319a5840e1944f3ffd631c86e2072937ac60deae686670df1b3fa178dfb" :answer-field/choices ["q-81557/selection/513f9fe6044a27d7e7c75e28e0903cb1a8f7514cae2f17d52773451e2a33062d" "q-81557/selection/2c0500ace50320340c88d141436938707d74974b748a1ba1daa9ad6ee96b8250" "q-81557/selection/942a7319a5840e1944f3ffd631c86e2072937ac60deae686670df1b3fa178dfb" "q-81557/selection/90c96fbe037c4fb273d63857e8c5e539fea154d4e96d956c702836bbdb448824" "q-81557/selection/b8d4d103913bf9d4df9ce0b9cd9b27cd4be59baeb236890bf50b08497b3c115e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81557" :question/id #uuid "32ece69d-c2b4-54a3-8cdb-0af4b72c6b7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81557" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi x}{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+n \\\\ x & =1+2n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=1+2n,$ where $n$ is an integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-81557/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8854eaba-96c7-5ea8-a071-7ff7c954f943"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81557"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
